\title{Counterfactual Number Theory: Which Arithmetic Laws Survive a Deformation of the Primes?}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We investigate a counterfactual arithmetic in which the notion of \emph{which numbers are prime} is deformed while the ambient multiplicative structure of the natural numbers is left untouched. We replace the ordinary primes by the irreducible elements of the \emph{Hilbert monoid} $H = \{\, n \in \N : n \equiv 1 \pmod 4 \,\}$, a classical multiplicatively closed subset of the naturals whose ``primes'' are those members admitting no nontrivial factorization \emph{within} $H$. Using this toy model we establish a sharp dividing line between arithmetic laws that are robust under such a deformation and those that are fragile. We prove: (1) $H$ is a submonoid of $(\N, \cdot)$, so the multiplicative skeleton survives; (2) there are infinitely many $H$-irreducibles, obtained from the rational primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ via Dirichlet's theorem, so infinitude of primes survives; and (3) unique factorization collapses, witnessed by the explicit minimal identity $441 = 9 \cdot 49 = 21 \cdot 21$ with $9, 21, 49$ all $H$-irreducible and $\{9,49\} \neq \{21,21\}$ as multisets. We argue that the collapse is a structural consequence of admitting a proper subgroup of residues, and we outline conjectures extending the closure/collapse dichotomy to general congruence monoids and to randomized prime systems.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The primes and the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic are so intertwined in elementary number theory that it is easy to conflate them. Yet many classical theorems about the primes---Euclid's infinitude, Dirichlet's theorem on arithmetic progressions, the Prime Number Theorem---are \emph{distributional} statements about how primes are spread through the integers, while the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic is a \emph{structural} statement about how integers decompose. This paper asks a deliberately naive question to separate these two flavors: if we keep the natural numbers and their multiplication but change which numbers count as prime, which classical theorems survive and which collapse?

We make the question precise by choosing a specific deformation. We work inside the \emph{Hilbert monoid}
\[
H = \{\, n \in \N : n \equiv 1 \pmod 4 \,\} = \{1, 5, 9, 13, 17, 21, 25, 29, 33, 37, 41, 45, 49, \dots\},
\]
a set introduced by Hilbert precisely to demonstrate that unique factorization can fail. The ``primes'' of this world are its irreducible elements---numbers in $H$ that cannot be written as a product of two smaller elements \emph{of $H$}. Because a factor is only legal if it itself lies in $H$, numbers like $9 = 3 \cdot 3$ become irreducible: their only rational factor $3$ is exiled from $H$.

This is a faithful, fully computable model of a counterfactual number theory. The ambient arithmetic is unchanged; only the notion of primality is deformed, by remembering just the residue class modulo $4$. Our main contribution is to isolate a clean dichotomy: coarse laws (multiplicative closure, infinitude of primes) survive, while fine laws (unique factorization) collapse, already at the minimal witness $441$. We further explain why the collapse is forced: it is governed by the index of the admitted residues inside the unit group $(\Z/4\Z)^{\times}$.

\subsection{Related notions}
The Hilbert monoid is the simplest nontrivial \emph{congruence monoid}: for a modulus $m$ and a submonoid $G$ of $(\Z/m\Z)^{\times}$ (here $m=4$, $G=\{1\}$), one forms $M(G) = \{n : n \bmod m \in G\}$. Congruence monoids are a well-studied source of non-unique factorization phenomena and of half-factorial and elasticity invariants. We use their smallest instance as a laboratory separating robust from fragile arithmetic laws.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\N = \{0, 1, 2, \dots\}$ and all factorizations are of positive integers.

\begin{definition}[Hilbert monoid]
The \emph{Hilbert monoid} is given by the predicate $\inH(n) \iff n \bmod 4 = 1$, and $H = \{ n \in \N : \inH(n) \}$ is the set of natural numbers congruent to $1$ modulo $4$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$H$-irreducible]
A natural number $n$ is \emph{$H$-irreducible} (a \emph{counterfactual prime}) if
\[
n \geq 2, \quad \inH(n), \quad \text{and} \quad \forall a,b \in \N \ \big( \inH(a) \wedge \inH(b) \wedge ab = n \implies a = 1 \vee b = 1 \big).
\]
\end{definition}

The essential feature is that the quantifier ranges over factorizations \emph{inside} $H$. Quantifying over all of $\N$ would make almost every composite reducible and render the model vacuous; restricting to $H$ deforms primality nontrivially.

\begin{remark}
Since $1 \bmod 4 = 1$, we have $\inH(1)$: the unit lies in the monoid, and is excluded from irreducibility by $n \geq 2$.
\end{remark}

\section{The multiplicative skeleton survives}

\begin{theorem}[Closure]\label{thm:closure}
The Hilbert monoid is a submonoid of $(\N, \cdot)$: it contains $1$, and if $\inH(a)$ and $\inH(b)$ then $\inH(ab)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
First, $1 \bmod 4 = 1$, so $\inH(1)$. For closure, if $a \equiv 1$ and $b \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, then
\[
ab \bmod 4 = \big( (a \bmod 4)(b \bmod 4) \big) \bmod 4 = (1 \cdot 1) \bmod 4 = 1,
\]
so $\inH(ab)$.
\end{proof}

This is the bedrock: multiplication never escapes $H$, so factorization \emph{within} the counterfactual world is meaningful. The multiplicative structure is completely robust.

\section{Counterfactual primes: explicit irreducibles}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:small}
Each of $9$, $21$, and $49$ is $H$-irreducible.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each of $9 = 4\cdot 2 + 1$, $21 = 4\cdot 5 + 1$, $49 = 4\cdot 12 + 1$ lies in $H$ and is $\geq 2$. Suppose $ab = n$ with $\inH(a), \inH(b)$, $n \in \{9, 21, 49\}$. Then $a \mid n$ and $a \leq n$, ranging over finitely many divisors.
For $9$, the divisors are $1, 3, 9$; only $1, 9 \in H$ since $3 \equiv 3$. For $21$, the divisors are $1, 3, 7, 21$; only $1, 21 \in H$. For $49$, the divisors are $1, 7, 49$; only $1, 49 \in H$. In each case one factor is $1$.
\end{proof}

The ordinary prime factors $3$ and $7$ both lie in the class $3 \pmod 4$, outside $H$; with those factors forbidden, $9$, $21$, $49$ have no legal nontrivial decomposition.

\begin{lemma}[Rational primes $\equiv 1$ import as counterfactual primes]\label{lem:import}
If $p$ is a rational prime with $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, then $p$ is $H$-irreducible.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Since $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, $\inH(p)$ and $p \geq 2$. If $ab = p$ with $\inH(a), \inH(b)$, then $a \mid p$, so $a = 1$ or $a = p$; if $a = p$ then $b = 1$. Either way one factor is $1$.
\end{proof}

\section{Infinitude of primes survives}

\begin{theorem}[Dirichlet, progression $1 \bmod 4$]\label{thm:dirichlet}
There are infinitely many rational primes $p$ with $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$.
\end{theorem}

This is the case $m = 4$, residue $1$ of Dirichlet's theorem; it also follows from a Euclid-style argument using that odd prime divisors of $N^2 + 1$ are $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$.

\begin{theorem}[Infinitude of counterfactual primes]\label{thm:infinite}
The set $\{ n \in \N : n \text{ is } H\text{-irreducible} \}$ is infinite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:dirichlet} there are infinitely many rational primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:import} each is $H$-irreducible. Hence the set of $H$-irreducibles contains an infinite subset and is infinite.
\end{proof}

Euclid's infinitude of primes is robust: it survives because it is fed directly by a distributional theorem indifferent to the fine structure of factorization.

\section{Unique factorization collapses}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of unique factorization]\label{thm:collapse}
In the counterfactual world $H$, unique factorization into $H$-irreducibles fails. Explicitly, $9$, $21$, $49$ are $H$-irreducible, and
\[
441 = 9 \cdot 49 = 21 \cdot 21,
\]
with the two factorizations genuinely distinct: $\{9, 49\} \neq \{21, 21\}$ as multisets.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Irreducibility is Lemma~\ref{lem:small}. The identities $9 \cdot 49 = 441$ and $21 \cdot 21 = 441$ are immediate, and $441 = 4 \cdot 110 + 1 \in H$. The multisets differ since $21 \notin \{9, 49\}$; this is not a reordering but two structurally different products.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Minimality]
$441 = 21^2$ is the smallest number in $H$ with two distinct factorizations into $H$-irreducibles. Such a witness must be built from exiled residue-$3$ primes bundled in pairs; $3$ and $7$ are the two smallest, and the smallest number pairing them two ways is $(3\cdot 3)(7 \cdot 7) = (3 \cdot 7)(3\cdot 7) = 441$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Why the collapse is structural]
Let $U = (\Z/4\Z)^{\times} = \{1, 3\}$. The Hilbert monoid admits only $G = \{1\}$, of index $2$ in $U$. The exiled residue $3$ cannot appear alone but reappears in pairs ($3\cdot 3 \equiv 1$, $3 \cdot 7 \equiv 1$), so exiled primes re-bundle into $H$-irreducibles in more than one way, and uniqueness fails. Had $G = U$ (index $1$), no residue would be exiled and factorization would remain unique. The index $[U:G]$ is the controlling invariant.
\end{remark}

\section{Discussion: coarse versus fine arithmetic laws}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Classical law & Status in $H$ & Character \\
\midrule
Multiplicative closure / monoid structure & Survives (Thm.~\ref{thm:closure}) & Coarse \\
Infinitude of primes & Survives (Thm.~\ref{thm:infinite}) & Coarse \\
Unique factorization (FTA) & Collapses (Thm.~\ref{thm:collapse}) & Fine \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\emph{Which numbers are prime} is a fragile datum. Statements depending only on closure and abundance of primes are portable across deformed arithmetics; unique factorization depends on the precise identity of the primes and is the first casualty. This reframes the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic as a special gift of the full integers. A natural quantitative measure is \emph{elasticity}, the supremum over reducible elements of the ratio of longest to shortest factorization length; larger index is expected to drive elasticity upward.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Algorithm A (Membership/closure).} Test $n \equiv 1 \pmod 4$; verify $ab \in H$ for $a, b \in H$. Cost $O(1)$.

\textbf{Algorithm B ($H$-irreducibility).} For $n \in H$, $n \geq 2$, scan divisors $2 \leq a < n$; return irreducible iff no $a$ has $a, n/a \in H$. Cost $O(\sqrt n)$.

\textbf{Algorithm C (Search for non-unique factorizations).} For $n \in H$ up to $N$, compute all factorizations into $H$-irreducibles by recursive descent, collect as multisets, report $n$ with $\geq 2$ distinct multisets; rediscovers $441$ as the least witness.

\section{Applications and connections}

(1) A minimal pedagogical separation showing infinitude of primes and unique factorization are logically independent. (2) The base case of non-unique factorization theory for congruence monoids, with invariants such as elasticity and sets of lengths. (3) A link to generalized (Beurling) prime systems via Dirichlet series $\sum_{n \in H} n^{-s}$ and the existence of an Euler product as an analytic fingerprint of unique factorization.

\section{Future work}

We highlight four directions. \emph{A closure/collapse dichotomy for congruence monoids}: $M(G)$ is closed with infinitely many irreducibles, failing unique factorization exactly when $G$ is proper, with $[(\Z/m\Z)^{\times} : G]$ controlling. \emph{Elasticity grows with the index}, diverging along suitable moduli. \emph{A zeta-function criterion}: an Euler product of classical shape exists iff unique factorization holds. \emph{Randomized primes}: for $S \subseteq \N$ with each $n$ included independently with probability $\sim 1/\log n$, Dirichlet-type statements survive almost surely while unique factorization has no reason to hold.

\section{Conclusion}

Deforming primality to the irreducibles of $H = \{ n \equiv 1 \pmod 4 \}$ yields a clean separation: the multiplicative skeleton and infinitude of primes survive, while unique factorization collapses at the minimal witness $441 = 9 \cdot 49 = 21 \cdot 21$. The collapse is controlled by the index of admitted residues in the unit group. Counterfactual number theory thus measures which of arithmetic's laws are load-bearing and which are luxuries of the integers we inhabit.

